1.41.m:
The Sūta said:
Kailāsa has secluded, beautiful peaks, is adorned with wings [of foliage], and has the lustre of a conch;
it is the abode of the devotees of the gods, of those whose souls have accumulated merit.

1.43.m:
Ten thousand years is proclaimed as their lifespan;
and among those great-souled ones there is no distinction of dharma and adharma;
for non-violence and truthful speech exist by their very nature.

1.45.m:
The Sūta said:
This inherent nature of the Kurus has been correctly explained;
now I shall declare the inherent nature of Bhārata; learn of it.

The continent named Bhārata is where this Bhārata race lives;
and because he sustains (bharaṇa) the creatures, the Manu is called Bharata;
and from the statement of etymology, that continent is remembered as Bhārata.

1.46.m:
After its name, the lord of the forest in Jambūdvīpa is proclaimed.
A thousand and another hundred yojanas is this great tree;
the height of the king of trees touches the sky on every side.
